Thinking process
Medicare ERP — Version 1.0.0
This is the first release of Medicare ERP, a hospital management system designed to digitize the day-to-day operations of a healthcare facility. It was built over roughly four weeks, from mid-March to mid-April 2026.

What It Does
The system covers the entire patient journey from the moment they walk into the hospital to the moment they pay their bill and leave. A patient is registered, placed in a queue, triaged by a nurse, seen by a doctor, sent for lab tests or prescriptions if needed, and finally billed at the cashier's desk.

Who Uses It
The system supports different types of hospital staff — doctors, secretaries, lab technicians, pharmacists, cashiers, and administrators. Each person only sees the parts of the system relevant to their job. An administrator can manage everything, while a secretary might only handle patient registration and appointments.

Main Modules
Authentication — Staff log in with an email and password. The system enforces security measures like rate-limited login attempts and secure session handling.
Patient Registration — Recording patient demographics and creating their hospital profile.
Appointments — Scheduling patient visits with doctors.
Queue Management — Tracking which patients are waiting and in what order they should be seen.
Clinical Desk — Where nurses record vitals and doctors conduct consultations and write clinical notes.
Laboratory — Doctors can order lab tests from a predefined catalog. Lab technicians process the tests and enter results. The system tracks which supplies are consumed per test.
Pharmacy — Managing drug stock and dispensing medication to patients, functioning like a point-of-sale system.
Bed Management — Tracking which hospital beds are available, occupied, or reserved for inpatients.
Billing — Generating invoices that pull charges from consultations, lab tests, and pharmacy dispensing into one bill. Supports mobile money payments through M-PESA.
Inventory — General inventory tracking across multiple hospital locations, with batch-level stock management.
Analytics and Reports — Dashboards and reports for administrators to monitor hospital performance.
User Management — Administrators can create staff accounts, assign roles, and control permissions.
Real-Time Notifications — When something happens in one department (like a lab result being completed), other relevant staff are notified instantly without needing to refresh their screen.
How It Is Built
The backend is a Python web server and the frontend is a React web application. The database is PostgreSQL. The backend is hosted on Render and the frontend is deployed on Vercel, making it accessible from any browser.

What Comes Next
The roadmap includes insurance and scheme management, advanced financial features, and a dedicated triage module — none of which are part of this first release.
